+## Terminal callback ownership
+
+`DevContext.callbacks` owns the three notification closures separately from the
+immutable watch/build options. Construction moves the callback fields out of
+`NormalizedDevOptions`; notification sites take a short-lived `Arc` snapshot and
+invoke it after releasing the callback-store lock. The immutable options do not
+keep a duplicate callback owner.
+
+Only the first, terminal `DevEngine::close` releases that store. It first marks
+the engine closed, sends `Close`, takes any not-yet-started coordinator, closes
+the bundler, and waits for the running coordinator without holding either mutex.
+Ordinary close errors are propagated only after cleanup and callback release.
+A concurrent repeated close keeps its existing no-op behavior and must not
+release callbacks while the first close is still pending.
+
+`run` checks the closed flag while holding the coordinator-state lock, so it
+cannot start a producer after terminal close captured its join handle. Lazy
+compilation rechecks the closed flag after acquiring the bundler lock. An
+already-admitted lazy compile holds that lock through its additional-assets
+notification; a queued late compile rejects instead of emitting after release.
+These are the producer barriers that make callback release safe.
+
+In the N-API binding, dropping the last `JsCallback` normally releases its TSFN.
+Normal release preserves already-enqueued JS calls until the queue is empty; it
+does not abort them. Native close waits for producers to finish enqueueing, not
+for user callback promises. Waiting for those promises could deadlock when a
+callback awaits `close()` itself. A queued callback can therefore still run after
+the close promise resolves; its output payload and JS callback remain owned by
+the queue until delivery. Environment teardown has the separate Node-API
+shutdown semantics.
+
+Callback release does not destroy `DevEngine`, clear its public `moduleGraph`
+handle, or change the existing graph/read results. Public handles may still own
+the closed native engine; they no longer keep user notification callbacks alive.
+Existing plugin/options ownership is governed by its own lifecycle.
